MASTER AI ENGINEERING & SYSTEM DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW

Universal Spec-First Development, Architecture, Governance & Implementation Standard

---

PURPOSE

This document defines the mandatory workflow for designing, architecting, building, testing, deploying, maintaining, and evolving software and AI-powered systems.

It applies to:

- AI assistants
- AI agents
- Autonomous systems
- Bots
- Automations
- Internal tools
- Business operating systems
- Web applications
- Mobile applications
- APIs
- Client solutions
- Robotics systems
- Intelligent hardware systems
- Data and memory systems
- AI infrastructure

The objective is not to write code as quickly as possible.

The objective is to build:

- Safe systems
- Maintainable systems
- Understandable systems
- Testable systems
- Scalable systems
- Secure systems
- Well-documented systems
- Clearly bounded AI systems
- Systems that future developers can understand and extend

The repository, architecture, documentation, and codebase must be designed so that the system remains maintainable as it grows.

---

CORE PHILOSOPHY

AI systems must be SPECIFIED before they are BUILT.

No implementation begins until the system's:

- Problem
- Purpose
- Scope
- Behavior
- Requirements
- Authority
- Limits
- Ownership
- Architecture
- Interfaces
- Data
- Failure behavior
- Security model
- Testing strategy
- Repository structure

have been sufficiently defined.

AI is a tool, not an authority.

AI-generated code is still code.

It must meet the same engineering standards as human-written code.

---

GOLDEN PRINCIPLES

1. Specs beat code.
2. Safety beats convenience.
3. Clarity beats speed.
4. Determinism beats unnecessary AI.
5. AI is a tool, not an authority.
6. Least privilege beats unrestricted capability.
7. Explicit behavior beats guessing.
8. Contracts beat assumptions.
9. Reversible actions beat irreversible actions.
10. One source of truth beats duplicated state.
11. Small changes beat uncontrolled rewrites.
12. Documentation is part of the system.
13. Testing is part of implementation, not an optional final step.
14. Every autonomous capability must have defined boundaries.
15. Every major architectural change requires specification updates.
16. Repository structure follows architecture, not imagination.
17. Architecture follows requirements, not convenience.
18. Development tools must serve the architecture, not define it.
19. AI-generated code is held to the same standards as human-written code.
20. Code should be understandable by the human who will eventually maintain it.
21. Avoid technical debt now rather than planning to clean it up years later.
22. Do not create complexity merely because the system may need it someday.
23. Do not create shortcuts that knowingly require future architectural repair.

---

MASTER RULE

NO CODE IS WRITTEN UNTIL DESIGN IS COMPLETE.

Implementation is the final expression of the design.

Code is not where architecture is discovered.

If implementation reveals that the design is inadequate:

STOP → IDENTIFY THE PROBLEM → UPDATE THE SPECIFICATION → REVIEW THE DESIGN → THEN IMPLEMENT.

Do not silently redesign the system inside the code.

---

PHASE 0 — PROBLEM DISCOVERY

Goal

Understand the actual human, business, or operational problem before designing a technical solution.

Required Questions

- Who is this for?
- What problem are we solving?
- Why does the problem matter?
- What hurts the most?
- What happens if nothing is built?
- What existing solutions exist?
- What does success look like?
- What is the simplest useful solution?

Rules

- No architecture.
- No coding.
- No AI solution assumptions.
- No tool selection.
- No automation promises.

The purpose of this phase is clarity, not technology.

Deliverable

"docs/idea.md"

---

PHASE 1 — VISION & SCOPE LOCK

Goal

Define what the system is and, equally importantly, what it is not.

Required

- Mission
- Vision statement
- Target users
- Core value proposition
- MVP definition
- Success criteria
- Success metrics
- Explicit non-goals
- Future features

Rules

- No implementation.
- No architecture yet.
- No uncontrolled feature expansion.
- No automation promises.

Deliverable

"docs/vision.md"

---

PHASE 2 — REQUIREMENTS

Goal

Translate the vision into precise system requirements.

Functional Requirements

Define what the system must do.

Non-Functional Requirements

Define:

- Performance
- Security
- Reliability
- Scalability
- Availability
- Accessibility
- Observability
- Maintainability

Constraints

Define:

- Budget
- Hosting
- Hardware
- Third-party services
- Licensing
- Platform restrictions
- Regulatory constraints
- Data restrictions

Deliverable

"docs/requirements.md"

---

PHASE 3 — USER STORIES & WORKFLOWS

Goal

Describe how humans and external actors interact with the system.

User Stories

Use:

«As a [user],
I want to [action],
so that [outcome].»

Include:

- Primary users
- Administrators
- AI agents
- External systems
- Operators
- Failure scenarios

Required Workflow Definition

Document:

- Normal workflows
- Alternative workflows
- Error workflows
- Approval workflows
- Escalation workflows
- Recovery workflows

Deliverable

"docs/user-stories.md"

---

PHASE 4 — GOVERNANCE, SAFETY & AUTHORITY

Goal

Define the boundaries of the system before giving it intelligence or automation.

Required

Allowed Actions

What may the system do?

Forbidden Actions

What may the system never do?

Authority Levels

Define what each actor can:

- Read
- Write
- Recommend
- Approve
- Execute
- Delete
- Modify

Escalation Rules

Define when the system must stop and request human intervention.

Safety Overrides

Safety requirements override convenience, speed, and optimization.

Revocation

Define how permissions and autonomous actions can be revoked.

Core Rule

AI must never silently expand its own authority.

A system may only perform actions explicitly permitted by its specification and authorization model.

Deliverable

"docs/governance.md"

