STOP → IDENTIFY THE UNCERTAINTY → CHECK THE SPECIFICATION → ASK OR ESCALATE.

Do not guess.

---

CHANGE CONTROL

A change is required to pass through the appropriate level of review.

Minor Change

May proceed within the existing specification if it does not alter:

- Architecture
- Security
- Contracts
- Authority
- Data model
- User-facing requirements

Significant Change

Requires specification review when it changes:

- Requirements
- Architecture
- APIs
- Data model
- AI behavior
- Agent authority
- Security
- External integrations
- Repository structure

Major Change

Requires re-evaluation of the affected lifecycle phases.

Examples:

- New autonomous capability
- New agent
- New database architecture
- New external system
- Major security change
- New user class
- Major workflow change
- Major repository restructuring

---

FINAL ENGINEERING STANDARD

The objective is not to write code.

The objective is to build maintainable, safe, understandable systems efficiently.

The repository must be understandable.

The architecture must be intentional.

The code must have a proper home.

The AI must have boundaries.

The human must retain authority.

The documentation must reflect reality.

The tests must protect behavior.

The contracts must protect interfaces.

The system must be designed for its future maintainer—not merely its current builder.

FINAL PIPELINE

PROBLEM → VISION → REQUIREMENTS → USER STORIES → GOVERNANCE → ARCHITECTURE → CONTRACTS → DETAILED DESIGN → SERVICE SPECS → INTELLIGENCE → AUTOMATION → RUNTIME → REPOSITORY DESIGN → ROADMAP → TASKS → IMPLEMENTATION → TESTING → REVIEW → DEPLOYMENT → POSTMORTEM → ITERATION

MASTER RULE

SPECIFY → DESIGN → GOVERN → CONTRACT → STRUCTURE → IMPLEMENT → TEST → REVIEW → DEPLOY → LEARN → REPEAT

Code is the final expression of the design—not the place where the design is discovered.
